MANDATORY GEOMETRY: place the BASES / UNDERSIDES of every cloud near the OUTER CIRCUMFERENCE. Their rounded growing TOPS point RADIALLY INWARD toward the EXACT IMAGE CENTER. Imagine individual upright clouds placed around a clock and rotated so their feet touch its circumference and their heads point at clock center. Each cloud is tangentially long, radially tall. At 3 o'clock the cloud has its broad flat dark BASE on RIGHT, bright rounded tower tips on LEFT. At 1:30 the base is NORTHEAST, rounded tower tips SOUTHWEST. At 5 o'clock base SOUTHEAST, tower tips NORTHWEST. At 9 o'clock base LEFT, tips RIGHT. At 6 o'clock base BELOW, tips ABOVE. NO cloud keeps screen-up orientation unless it lies at 6 o'clock.
Asymmetric reference composition: one dominant connected massive cloud bank wrapping the RIGHT quadrant from 1:30 to 5 o'clock, with only three or four broad major rounded crown lobes extending toward center. A few shallow distant banks at 8 to 10 o'clock, their tiny crowns also point inward. Mostly empty black center and upper-left, no wreath, no giant left crescent. Keep all clouds inset from frame.
